MID: 8bd6eaef94959f7fada84b2c9309e995
STATEMENT: >>>
Run from the separate bootstrap SSH account after host inspection and before application initialization. That account needs the declared Ansible privilege escalation to root; the rootless service account does not receive sudo. Ansible switches to the service account for all Podman and user-systemd operations, and uses root only for the protected PostgreSQL initializer under `/etc/codexia`. Use a private inventory and a reviewed checkout. Run the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-ANSIBLE-BASE-SECRETS, `secrets` stage] first.

```sh
ansible-playbook -i /private/codexia-inventory.yml deploy/ansible/site.yml \
  -e ansible_user=codexia-bootstrap \
  -e codexia_stage=base -e codexia_base_approved=true
```

The stage adopts exact existing Podman network, volume, PostgreSQL and canonical Tunnel identities. It installs only missing fixed `.network`, `.volume`, `.container` and PostgreSQL initializer files. A noncanonical existing file, network isolation setting, volume layout, DB container mount, database role, migration ledger or actual Tunnel image, network, loopback-only port or secret mount fails closed before any resource is changed. The stage never deletes a resource, overwrites a Quadlet, changes a database password or runs schema migrations. `prepare` then installs the reviewed sources using the unprivileged service account.

PostgreSQL adoption requires the pinned PostgreSQL 17 image, the canonical `codexia-postgres` volume mounted at `/var/lib/postgresql/data` with `PGDATA=/var/lib/postgresql/data/pgdata`, the expected init-script bind, the internal-only database network and no published ports. A nonempty cluster must include core PostgreSQL control files. If `codexia_schema_migrations` exists, its version/checksum map must exactly match the migrations in the reviewed checkout. Without a ledger, adoption is allowed only when the application database contains no user schema objects.

When the canonical PostgreSQL volume is absent or empty, the stage requires a separate explicit approval before starting a fresh cluster:

```sh
ansible-playbook -i /private/codexia-inventory.yml deploy/ansible/site.yml \
  -e ansible_user=codexia-bootstrap \
  -e codexia_stage=base -e codexia_base_approved=true \
  -e codexia_database_initialization_approved=true
```

This approval also permits recovery of a missing `codexia` login only when the database has no application objects and its owner remains the default `codexia_admin`. Recovery creates the role with the already managed app secret and transfers database ownership in one transaction. If an existing role cannot authenticate with that secret, the stage stops without rotating it. An existing DB container is verified in place and is not restarted. The pinned Tunnel unit is installed but not started; its dependency remains attached to the currently installed API until the separately approved initialization transition.

The stage is safe to repeat after matching convergence. If fresh initialization is interrupted, retain the existing volume and all secrets, keep the initialization approval in the protected inventory, inspect the actual state, and rerun the stage. A schema, role, source or credential mismatch that is not the explicit empty-database recovery case requires operator diagnosis; do not delete or recreate resources to force convergence.

After ACTIVE, the protected base operation can also provision the [LINK: DOC-DEPLOYMENT-BACKUP-CONSOLE-OPERATIONS, VM backup runtime]. This requires the separate backup opt-in. It configures the private authenticated socket and backup journal, then attaches the Console and starts its restricted worker.

CI exercises fresh and repeated convergence, existing-volume adoption, migration-ledger drift, role recovery, major-version rejection and the guarded Tunnel dependency switch on a disposable Ubuntu 26.04 rootless Podman/systemd VM. Those synthetic tests are not proof of production adoption, backup or restore.
<<<
